[h1]Speculative Markets - Beta[/h1]
The testing channel for Speculative Markets, a Victoria 3 mod about investment, booms, financial panics, and the policies used to manage them.

[b]Enable either this Beta or the stable mod, never both.[/b] Beta is a complete mod, not an add-on. Disable stable manually before enabling Beta.

Changes are tested here before promotion to stable. Use a separate playset and keep copies of saves when trying experimental revisions.

[h2]What's in this beta[/h2]
[b]Competing Exchanges[/b]
Compete with any Exchange using the compete button in the Leading Exchanges list, as many as you can afford. Each competitor costs influence by its Standing and strains relations. A contest is a tug of war settled every 5 years: the bar starts from the two scores, and every action that works pushes it your way. A result within 20% of the middle is a draw. A decisive win needs 60%. A win takes trust from the loser; a decisive win also takes part of their investment pool income for a year. This is separate from vanilla's diplomatic rivalries.

Act through Bear Raid, Lure Their Capital, Press Campaign, Hold the Line and Shore Up. Costs and effects scale with the two Exchanges' sizes. The Competition section in the Exchanges tab shows the Contest bar for each competitor and the moves running against you. AI Exchanges pick competitors they can beat and play each contest.

[b]World Centre for the top three[/b]
The top three Exchanges are World Centres, with cheaper loans, more influence, smaller losses from foreign panics and one competitor free of upkeep. The Exchange score now favors size less, so other great powers can compete with the leader.

[b]Paths to Renown and specialities[/b]
Three new charter paths: Futures Pit, Lender to Nations and Railway Mania. Every path now has a drawback that grows with your place, and each pulls levers in other systems as a trade-off. The Renown screen shows rewards on the leader flags and the effects of locked paths.

[b]Blue Chips and Company of the Year[/b]
A company that stays prosperous for a year becomes a blue chip. Each one, up to 5, softens the confidence lost to collapsing booms and foreign panics and pays your Exchange a little trust every year. Events let you cash in a new blue chip or prop up one that slips. Each year the world's most prosperous blue chip is named Company of the Year, with trust and prestige for its country. Each blue chip, up to 5, also gives +0.6% Capitalist investment pool contribution and -1% loan interest (Blue Chip Index), and moves every contest with a competing Exchange 3% your way. A Bear Raid that lands rattles one of the target's blue chips, so for a year one month below 70 prosperity loses the status.

The Finance tabs are now Trading Floor, Speculation, Banking and Exchanges.

[h2]Latest update[/h2]
[b]1.0.6-beta.12[/b]
Covers every change since stable 1.0.6. A rival Exchange named in 1.0.6 is now a competitor. Beta.11's, which came from diplomatic rivalries, no longer count. The full list is in the change notes.

[url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3831434383]Prefer the regular release? Use Speculative Markets here.[/url]

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]
[list]
[*]Using CMF? Add the [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3839275348]CMF patch[/url] and load it last.
[*]Using Morgenroete? Add the [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3839705018]Morgenroete patch[/url]. Disable the old Speculative Markets+Morgenroete patch.
[/list]
With both, use this order: [b]CMF > Morgenroete > Speculative Markets > Morgenroete patch > CMF patch.[/b] You can use Beta in place of Speculative Markets. Don't enable both.

[h2]Reporting problems[/h2]
Include the beta version, game version, relevant mods and load order, and the steps that reproduce the problem. For Finance shortcuts, say which panel you opened from and which tab appeared. A screenshot or save from before the issue can help reproduce it.

Built against Victoria 3 1.15.1. This is the separate testing distribution of Speculative Markets; it is not Community Mod Framework itself.
